* Week 10: TABLE() and indexing with a non-integer key (research/
* snobol/02-language-reference.md, "Arrays and tables"): a table is
* keyed by any data object, not just integers, and grows dynamically
* [GreenBook1971 SS1.13].
        T = TABLE()
        T<'NAME'> = 'ADA'
        T<'YEAR'> = 1815
        OUTPUT = 'NAME=' T<'NAME'> ' YEAR=' T<'YEAR'>
* A missing key is a genuine failure, not a crash -- same treatment as
* an out-of-bounds array read.
        T<'MISSING'>                                 :S(BADKEY)
        OUTPUT = 'MISSING KEY CORRECTLY FAILED'
* Table identity: a value fetched from a table shares the same
* underlying mutable structure as the table's own copy, so mutating it
* through one variable is visible through a second, independent fetch
* of the same key -- OCaml never copies an array or a Hashtbl value on
* lookup.
        T<'ROW'> = ARRAY(3)
        ROW = T<'ROW'>
        ROW<1> = 99
        OUTPUT = 'VIA ROW: ROW<1>=' ROW<1>
        ROW2 = T<'ROW'>
        OUTPUT = 'VIA A FRESH FETCH: ROW2<1>=' ROW2<1>             :(END)
BADKEY    OUTPUT = 'UNREACHABLE (MISSING KEY)'
END       N = N
